Dagu host regression tests
============

These tests compile functions extracted from the kernel sources against small
userspace substitutes. They need Python 3, a native C compiler with sanitizer
support, and pthreads, plus make and tar. The legacy kselftest framework
also needs bc and rsync. They do not require root or access to device sysfs.
They do not validate kernel RCU, IPC priority restoration, reclaim progress,
boot, OOM behavior or device performance.

Run from the kernel source root:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=dagu run_tests

SANITIZERS defaults to undefined. Use SANITIZERS=address,undefined to also
check memory safety on hosts supporting AddressSanitizer. HOSTCC selects
the native compiler used for the extracted code.

Use a separate build directory with:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=dagu O=/tmp/dagu-build run_tests

The standard kselftest install target bundles the tested source files in
kernel-sources.tar, so installed tests do not need the original checkout:

  make -C tools/testing/selftests TARGETS=dagu install INSTALL_PATH=/tmp/dagu-tests
  /tmp/dagu-tests/run_kselftest.sh

Build the bundle again after changing a source file before installing tests.
In-tree runs read the live checkout; installed runs read their source bundle.
The shared extractor handles braces inside C strings, character literals and
comments, but is not a general-purpose C parser.

This kernel's legacy TAP runner reports failures as "not ok ... [FAIL]"
without setting a failing exit status. Consumers must check the TAP results;
the Dagu host checks workflow does so explicitly.

`test_limited_readahead.py` extracts the actual raw range and control parser
helpers. Literal contract examples, empty/overflow/async/marker and strict
input bounds are host arithmetic/interface checks only. Kernel CSS/RCU,
concurrency, IO and real policy decisions require a new experimental kernel;
see Documentation/vm/readahead-limit.rst.

`test_dagu_reclaim.py` also exercises the extracted slab submission and
registered enable setter: default synchronous fallback, invalid input,
pending/running drain waits, interrupted disable with admission left closed,
and a producer paused before the admission lock while disable completes.
Its worker completion, wait queues, freezer interruption and CSS references
are userspace substitutes; real memcg/freezer/reclaim validation remains
necessary on the target kernel.

`test_graphics_priority.py` extracts the restricted Binder graphics policy and
checks node inherit_rt allow/deny and absent buffer/node inputs, sender task
reference balance, receiver RT permission and stronger-priority preservation.
Its scheduler substitute observes boost requests only. It does not execute
Binder core node-minimum selection, nested transactions or reply/error recovery.
A node denying inheritance suppresses this extension's boost; Binder core may
still select an RT minimum configured on that node. Real IPC and recovery
remain separate device validation requirements.

The graphics check also exercises the opt-in official main-thread comm rules:
default-off, enabling/disabling subsequent requests, exact versus similar names,
main versus secondary threads, FIFO/RR versus normal senders, oneway/node/sender
gates and receiver permissions/equal or stronger RT protection. The parameter's
actual declaration supplies its default; the host check does not test the kernel
module-parameter sysfs parser or real in-flight disable/unload semantics.

mi_async_reclaim: test_mi_async_reclaim.py is a finite host admission and
ownership regression using actual C functions with stubs. It does not verify
real rmap contention, LRU accounting, worker execution or freezer/stop behavior.
See Documentation/vm/mi-async-reclaim.rst for the required real memory seam.

The mi_fault_around check extracts the control/decision C functions and uses
host adapters for kernel APIs. It covers boundaries, collision, error retention,
readback and switching; it does not validate real mmap/PTEs or concurrency.

mi_reclaim_pool: finite extracted actual owner acquire/release and complete
borrow entry check. Covers C21/C22 exclusivity, effective RT priority including
PI and excluding negative DL, GFP/context/worker/zone gates, pool-empty fallback
and page/vmstat ownership transfer. Kernel list, lock and task adapters are
limited host substitutes. Real direct-reclaim hook, cpuset/nodemask race, zero
and memcg, shrinker/refill and stop concurrency require device acceptance.
